      "text": "We use the property \\[\\log_b a(a^x) = x\\] to yield \\[\\log_b 8^7 (7^x) = 637.\\]Thus, $b = \\boxed{8}$.\n\nOR (No need to explain)\n\nLet's use \\[\\log_b 8^7 (7^x) = 637\\] to show that basically same thing $x=\\log_b8^7$, and cancelling the $8$ at the left of the equality yields $8^x=7^637$. But $7^3 = 343$, so $7^637 \\approx136,193,104$ is far far far less powerful of a base than $8^{637} \\approx 3.1 \\times 10^{2,197,452}$ ... so $b \\approx\\boxed{8}$.\n\nWell, you've got 2 problems. First: use the most common $\\log_b8^7x=637$ instead of $\\log_b8^x7$, and secondly, $8^{637}$ apparently is way too bigfor $7^{637}$.\n\nYes, IMHO that's better.",
