      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The base 7 exponentiation on the left and the base 8 exponentiation on the right must have the same exponent. Therefore, we may set $x + 7 = x$, however, this equation has no solution.\nWe rewrite the equation as $(7^x)^7 = 8^x$. This expands to $7^{7x} = 8^x$.\nLet us rewrite this equation in the desired form. To do so, let us take the logarithm base 7 of both sides: $\\log_7 7^{7x} = \\log_7 8^x$. The logarithm of a number to its base is just 1, which simplifies the previous equation to $7x = \\log_7 8^x$. In order to compare the bases, we must rewrite the base 7 exponent as an exponential expression with a base of b: $7^{\\log_7 7x} = 7^{\\log_7 b \\times \\log_7 8^x}$. The right side then becomes $b^{\\log_7 8^x}$. Therefore, we have $7x = \\log_7 b \\times \\log_7 8^x$.\nTo solve for $b$, we must raise both sides to the power that would \"cancel out\" the exponent, in this case, base 8. To do so, let us raise both sides to the power of $\\log_7 8$: $7^{\\log_7 8^2 x} = (b^{\\log_7 8^x})^{\\log_7 8}$. This simplifies to $7^x = b^{\\log_7 8^x}$. Therefore, $b = 7^7$.\nThe answer is 7",
