      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we can rewrite it using the properties of exponents and logarithms. Here's the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Start with the given equation:\n   \\[\n   7^{x+7} = 8^x\n   \\]\n\n2. Use the property of exponents \\(a^{m+n} = a^m \\cdot a^n\\) to rewrite the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   7^x \\cdot 7^7 = 8^x\n   \\]\n\n3. Isolate the term \\(7^x\\) on the left-hand side by dividing both sides by \\(7^x\\):\n   \\[\n   7^7 = \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)^x\n   \\]\n\n4. Recognize that \\(\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)^x\\) is the same as \\(8/x\\):\n   \\[\n   7^7 = \\frac{8}{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. Solve for \\(x\\) by expressing \\(7^7\\) as a power of 7:\n   \\[\n   x = \\log_7 \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. According to the problem, we are given that the solution can be expressed in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Therefore, we equate the two expressions for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   \\log_b 7^7 = \\log_7 \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n7. By the property of logarithms \\(\\log_b a = \\log_c a / \\log_c b\\), we can rewrite \\(\\log_7 (8/7)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\log_7 (8/7) = \\log_7 8 - \\log_7 7\n   \\]\n\n8. Simplify \\(\\log_7 7\\):\n   \\[\n   \\log_7 7 = 1\n   \\]\n\n9. Combine the expressions:\n   \\[\n   \\log_b 7^7 = \\log_7 8 - 1\n   \\]\n\n10. Solve for \\(b\\) by recognizing that the equation \\(\\log_b 7^7 = 1 - \\log_7 8\\) must hold true. This implies:\n    \\[\n    b^{1 - \\log_7 8} = 7^7\n    \\]\n\n11. Solve for \\(b\\):\n    \\[\n    b = 7^{7/\\log_7 8}\n    \\]\n\n12. Simplify the exponent:\n    \\[\n    b = 7^{7 \\log_8 7}\n    \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7^{7 \\log_8 7}}\n\\]",
